# From: (set authorship at submission)
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-alloc: check leafs in ggml_gallocr_needs_realloc

ggml_gallocr_needs_realloc validates every node and every node's direct
sources, but never the graph's leafs. A graph input consumed only through
views is sized at 0 on the consumer side (view_src set), so a leaf whose
allocation size grows between two topology-matching graphs is invisible to
the check: alloc_graph then assigns it the stored smaller offset, and any
full-extent write of the tensor legally overruns the next leaf's allocation.

Observed on Apple Metal with a DeepSeek-V4-class model: a sliding-window KQ
mask leaf stepping 131072 -> 262144 bytes (padded n_kv 256 -> 512) between
same-topology prefill graphs kept its old offset and overwrote the adjacent
inp_pos leaf with F16 -inf mask bytes, corrupting rope positions and every
downstream KV write, deterministically, whenever prompt chunking produced
topology-matching consecutive graphs.

Fix: mirror the existing node loop over the leafs.

---
 ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c
@@ ggml_gallocr_needs_realloc @@
     if (galloc->n_leafs != graph->n_leafs) {
 #ifndef NDEBUG
         GGML_LOG_DEBUG("%s: graph has different number of leafs\n", __func__);
 #endif
         return true;
     }
 
+    for (int i = 0; i < graph->n_leafs; i++) {
+        struct ggml_tensor * leaf = graph->leafs[i];
+        struct leaf_alloc * leaf_alloc = &galloc->leaf_allocs[i];
+
+        if (!ggml_gallocr_node_needs_realloc(galloc, leaf, &leaf_alloc->leaf)) {
+#ifndef NDEBUG
+            GGML_LOG_DEBUG("%s: leaf %s is not valid\n", __func__, leaf->name);
+#endif
+            return true;
+        }
+    }
+
     for (int i = 0; i < graph->n_nodes; i++) {
         struct ggml_tensor * node = graph->nodes[i];
         struct node_alloc * node_alloc = &galloc->node_allocs[i];
